-- title:
--   Reduced integral forms: poles of ̄ g¹²/Δ̄^k bounded by kord J
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, $\kappa$ a field of characteristic $p$, and $M \geq 1$ an integer with $p \nmid M$. Write $F =$ [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldC`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L134) $\kappa\, M$ for the intermediate field of $\kappa((q))$ over $\kappa$ generated by the ratios `intFormRatiosC` of level $\Gamma_1(M)$. Let $J \in F$ be an element whose underlying Laurent series is [`ModularCurve.jqModC`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L15) $\kappa$, namely $q^{-1}$ times the image in $\kappa[[q]]$ of the integral power series `jNum` $= E_4^3 \cdot$ `dedekindEtaUnitInv`, i.e. the reduction of the $q$-expansion of $j$. Let $k \in \mathbb{N}$, let $g$ be a modular form of weight $k$ on $\Gamma_1(M)$, and let $pg \in \mathbb{Z}[[q]]$ be a power series whose image in $\mathbb{C}[[q]]$ is the $q$-expansion of $g$; assume the image of $pg$ in $\kappa[[q]]$ is non-zero. Then there exists $T \in F$ whose Laurent series is the quotient of the reduction of $pg^{12}$ by the reduction of $(q \cdot$ `dedekindEtaUnit` $)^k$, the $k$-th power of the $q$-expansion of $\Delta$, and such that for every place $y$ of $F$ over $\kappa$ — a valuation subring of $F$ containing $\kappa$, distinct from $F$ and a principal ideal ring — one has $k \cdot \min(0, \operatorname{ord}_y J) \le \operatorname{ord}_y T$, where $\operatorname{ord}_y$ is the normalised additive valuation attached to $y$.
--
--   This is the $q$-expansion principle in the form of a bound on the divisor of the reduction of an integral modular form: off the cusps (where $\operatorname{ord}_y J \ge 0$) the function $\bar g^{12}/\bar\Delta^{k}$ is regular, while at a cusp its pole order is at most $k$ times the pole order of $\bar\jmath$, the bound being attained for $g = E_4$. It is used in the analysis of places of the function field of $X_1(M)_\kappa$, in particular in the results on vanishing and on divisibility by $12$ of $\operatorname{ord}_y T$, and in the study of integral weight-one forms in characteristic $p$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_exists_coe_eq_div_pow_and_mul_min_ord_le_ord_x1FunctionFieldC.lean

